---
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="Quick start"
  description="Inspect the local generation capabilities, connect Claude Code, then verify the MCP connection without making a model call."
  toc={[
    ["inspect", "Inspect capabilities"],
    ["claude", "Connect Claude Code"],
    ["connection", "Check the connection"],
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="inspect">Inspect capabilities</h2>
  <p>
    After <a href="/installation/">installation</a>, use discovery to see which
    generation operations Clink recognizes. Admission is checked again when you
    submit work.
  </p>
  <Code label="Terminal" code="clink generation-capabilities --json" />
  <h2 id="claude">Connect Claude Code</h2>
  <p>
    In the project directory where you use Claude Code, register the server. The
    local scope keeps this registration private to you in that project. The
    project argument binds the workspace explicitly.
  </p>
  <Code
    label="Terminal · project directory"
    code={
      'claude mcp add --scope local --transport stdio clink -- "$(uv tool dir --bin)/clink-mcp-server" --project "$PWD"\nclaude mcp get clink'
    }
  />
  <h2 id="connection">Check the connection</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Look for <strong>Connected</strong> in the command output.
    </li>
    <li>
      Open Claude Code in the same directory. Run <code>/mcp</code> and look for{" "}
      <code>clink</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Ask Claude to call Clink’s <code>version</code> tool and then{" "}
      <code>listmodels</code>, passing an empty object to each. These local
      checks need no native agent login.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <Code label="MCP tool arguments · version or listmodels" code="{}" />
  <p>
    To make a real call, use a compatible, authenticated agent and follow the{" "}
    <a href="/examples/">generation or consultation example</a>. Other MCP
    clients can use the <a href="/mcp/#register">stdio registration example</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
